WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:05.400
Captivating new characters, another awesome
mix tape, and a generous helping of heart

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.580
are just some of the things waiting for you
in the bundle of fun that is Guardians of

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.190
the Galaxy Vol. 2.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.110
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.420
Just before I kick off my review proper, I
want to let you know I'm running a Guardians

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.340
of the Galaxy Vol. 2 giveaway on all my new
Guardians 2 videos.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.600
For a chance to win, make sure you're subscribed
and leave a comment about the movie on this

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:24.600
video.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.590
I'll have full details of the prizes at the
end of my review!

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:31.410
Ok, as I mentioned before, as well as our
team of returning heroes, we also get a bunch

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:34.989
of new characters and actors, who I really
enjoyed seeing in the movie.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:40.290
As the insect-like alien, Mantis, Pom Klementieff
is a simply perfect addition to this universe,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:44.380
and gives a charmingly subtle performance
that really highlights her character's innocence,

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:49.399
good nature, and humour, and her scenes with
Drax, in particular, are an utter joy.

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:53.059
Elizabeth Debicki, who you'll have seen in
The Man From UNCLE and alongside Tom Hiddleston

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:57.719
in the TV series The Night Manager, gives
a superbly stately and condescending turn

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:02.350
which fits her character wonderfully as she
plays Ayesha, the High Priestess of the ever-so-arrogant

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.170
alien race, the Sovereign.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:09.180
And Kurt Russell is just fantastic as Ego
in all his forms, managing to make this crazy

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.719
character believable, and also establishing
a great on-screen rapport with Chris Pratt's

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:14.979
Star-Lord.

00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:18.659
Just like the first film, there's plenty of
irreverent humour in Vol. 2, a lot of which

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:23.180
lands very well, and there's a good chunk
of fun and thrilling action too, however this

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.509
time, there's also more emotional weight given
to the Guardians' story, with an emphasis

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:31.359
placed on family, whether that's the family
you were born into or the people you meet

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:32.640
along the way.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.929
And having got to know his main cast and their
characters well over the course of filming

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:40.819
the first movie, it feels like writer-director
James Gunn has honed this script so it pretty

00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:43.649
much plays to each actor's particular strengths.

00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:47.630
The fact that Drax has a love of the literal
as well as about as much tact as a ton of

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:52.719
bricks means that Dave Bautista gets a mass
of marvellously funny lines, which he nails

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:56.799
thanks to his great comic timing and delightfully
deadpan delivery.

00:01:56.799 --> 00:02:01.249
And then there's that hilariously hearty laugh
Bautista does as Drax which never fails to

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:05.009
put a smile on my face and you'll hear plenty
of throughout the film.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:10.119
As Peter Quill, aka Star-Lord, Chris Pratt
showcases a range of humour, action, and more

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:11.470
heartfelt, dramatic moments.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.689
While Zoe Saldana and Karen Gillan get some
cool action and the chance to peel back the

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:17.989
layers of Gamora and Nebula's backstory.

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:22.340
And Michael Rooker certainly makes the most
of the increased screen time Yondu gets this

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.250
time around, adding extra depth to his character.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.980
As for Baby Groot, he's a mix of ultra-cute
adorableness and a seething stack of rage,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.310
and the VFX guys have done superb job injecting
this tiny tree creature with a real sense

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:36.640
of soul and personality.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.700
I also really like Bradley Cooper's Rocket,
who gets a nice emotional arc this time too.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.160
Yes, there were odd moments of his humour
that didn't work for me, but he does still

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:50.700
get lots of great moments and I love Rocket
in all his fast-talking, sass-mouthed glory.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.459
As was the case with the first movie, the
soundtrack for Vol. 2 is a gorgeous mix that

00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:56.969
I'm looking forward to listening to again
and again.

00:02:56.969 --> 00:03:01.180
And as far as the movie's concerned, each
track gels beautifully with the scene it's

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.690
in, taking the on-screen visuals to another
level.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.370
Speaking of visuals taken to new heights,
the production design for this movie is out

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:08.370
of this world!

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:12.909
From the Sovereign's planet, which is all
Art Deco-style lavishness to Ego's home, which

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:17.420
perfectly reflects who his character is, the
work of the movie's art, design, and VFX teams

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:18.430
really shines.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.670
And, as you'd expect from a movie in the Marvel
Cinematic Universe, there are post-credits

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:27.659
scenes, five to be precise, so it's definitely
worth sticking around until the very end of

00:03:27.659 --> 00:03:28.659
the film.

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:31.640
Plus, there are also plenty of easter eggs
and references in the movie too.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.430
And I'll be making videos about those various
post-credits scenes and their significance

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.969
in the MCU as well as the movie's easter eggs
very shortly.

00:03:38.969 --> 00:03:43.610
To sum up: Guardians of the Galaxy Vol 2 is
entertainment of cosmic proportions, or to

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.590
put it another way, it was a pleasure to watch.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:50.079
Writer-director James Gunn blends plentiful
laughs with an emotional kick in the gut and

00:03:50.079 --> 00:03:53.659
a galaxy of scene-stealing performances from
cast old and new.

00:03:53.659 --> 00:03:57.409
And I cannot wait to see what Gunn has up
his sleeve for Vol. 3!

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:00.450
But in the meantime, I'm looking forward to
watching Vol. 2 again!

